Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Tube Seven Takes the Low Note
- Story & Context: The debut opens at 132 BPM with João programming a clipped tamborzão-derived pattern while Bia answers every second cycle with a two-stroke repinique call. Marina plays a single low rackett note immediately after the first kick cluster. On the next cycle João removes that kick entirely and lets the rackett occupy its place. The substitution becomes the song's structural hook: machine low hit, acoustic reed low hit, then one full vacancy.

Elisa marks every fourth cycle with a deadened vibraphone fifth. Caio adds one palm-muted baritone-guitar pickup before Marina's second phrase but stays out of the sub register. Halfway through, Marina overblows the rackett into a rough higher partial while João keeps the same drum pattern, making the arrangement seem to lift without adding a chord change.

The final sixteen bars remove synthesized sub entirely. Rackett, kick, repinique, and vibraphone must carry the physical weight on their own. The track ends with one pneumatic carrier sliding through an unused tube in the room microphone.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Close documentary image of an old brass pneumatic-tube junction marked 7, compact wooden rackett beside a red maintenance ledger, one silver vibraphone bar and a repinique stick in shallow focus, warm industrial light. No full lyrics; sparse spoken fragments limited to "tube seven," "take the low note," "leave one empty," and "send it through."

Track 2: Felt Bar Between Two Kicks
- Story & Context: João builds the EP's driest groove at 136 BPM using only kick, snare, clap, and one short tom. Between two recurring low kicks, Elisa inserts a prepared-vibraphone note damped almost immediately by felt. Marina is allowed to enter only after that metal attack dies. Her rackett phrase therefore begins inside a measured pocket defined by drum impact and decay rather than by bar lines.

Caio plays clipped chromatic dyads that approach the vibraphone pitch from below. Bia gradually lengthens her repinique calls from two strokes to five but never crosses Marina's breath windows. During the central break, João removes both kicks while preserving claps and repinique. Marina plays three low rackett bursts separated by complete silence, turning the historical wind instrument into the only bass source for eight bars.

The kicks return without riser, fill, or crash. Elisa leaves the final felt-bar position empty, and Marina does not enter, producing a deliberately missing phrase before the track cuts.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Extreme close-up of a vibraphone bar with a small strip of gray felt touching its edge, two kick events drawn on graph paper on either side, rackett finger holes visible below. Spoken fragments limited to "between the kicks," "wait for the metal," "air after felt," and "leave the last pocket empty."

Track 3: Pressure Gauge Reads Zero, Reed Still Speaks
- Story & Context: The closer begins with field audio of a dead pneumatic pressure gauge whose needle sits at zero. Marina enters alone with a breath-heavy rackett fundamental, proving that the room's obsolete machinery no longer supplies pressure but the player's lungs still do. João adds a sparse 128 BPM beat with unusually long gaps. Caio contributes isolated low guitar harmonics, while Elisa uses one bowed vibraphone tone at the end of every eight-bar section.

Bia's repinique calls become the piece's countdown device. She starts with six strokes, then five, then four, while Marina's phrases become shorter and more forceful. Instead of building toward a louder climax, João removes drum elements until only clap and one dry kick remain. The sub disappears first, then guitar, then vibraphone.

In the final passage, Marina plays one last rackett note with no electronic reinforcement. The note cracks slightly at the beginning, stabilizes, and stops abruptly when she releases the air. The room microphone captures the dead pressure gauge tapping once against its stop after the sound is gone.
